      content: "## ✍️ Model DBQ moves\n\n**Prompt:** *Evaluate the extent to which Black thinkers agreed about strategies for achieving freedom between 1850 and 1930.*\n\n**Contextualization paragraph (model):**\n> In the decades after 1850, Black Americans faced a shifting and often worsening situation. The Fugitive Slave Act (1850) and *Dred Scott* (1857) denied that Black people had rights the nation must respect; emancipation and Reconstruction briefly promised citizenship, but by the 1890s disfranchisement, *Plessy v. Ferguson* (1896) and a wave of lynching had stripped much of it away. After 1915, the Great Migration brought new opportunities in Northern cities, yet the violence of the Red Summer of 1919 showed that moving north did not end racial oppression. Each turn in these conditions forced Black thinkers to ask again whether freedom could be won inside the United States, and by what means.\n\n**Thesis (model):**\n> Black thinkers between 1850 and 1930 largely agreed on the goal of full freedom and dignity, but they disagreed sharply over means: whether to claim citizenship in the United States or build a homeland abroad, as in the debates between Douglass and Delany and between Du Bois and Garvey, and whether to defer or demand political rights, as Washington and Du Bois argued; those disagreements grew sharpest when hopes for citizenship collapsed.\n\nNotice that the thesis **answers \"to what extent\"** (agreement on ends, disagreement on means), **names categories** for grouping documents, and **adds a causal claim** (context drives the debate) that the essay must prove.\n\n## ✍️ Model thematic SAQ (no source)\n\n**(a) Identify one way Pan-Africanism changed between 1900 and 1945.**\n> Pan-African meetings moved from polite petitions for reform under colonial rule, such as the 1900 London address, to the 1945 Manchester congress's demand for independence.\n\n**(b) Explain one reason for that change.**\n> World War II weakened European empires and brought a new generation of African leaders, such as Kwame Nkrumah, to the movement's center, so delegates in 1945 demanded self-rule instead of protection.\n\n## ⚠️ Mistakes that cost points\n\n- Crediting the **1900 London conference** to Du Bois. Henry Sylvester Williams organized it; Du Bois attended and drafted its address.\n- Calling Garvey's UNIA **only** a \"Back to Africa\" movement. Its core was race pride, enterprise and a free Africa.\n- Saying Du Bois **opposed industrial education**. He supported it for many but opposed giving up rights and higher education.\n- Saying King **rejected all self-defense**. He accepted individual self-defense and rejected organized violence as strategy.\n- Treating **intersectionality** as an idea born in 1989. Crenshaw named it; Cooper, the NACW and Combahee had described the problem earlier."
